  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 150 PM EDT Fri Oct 2 2026\n\nA cold front continues to slowly slide east this afternoon, with \nplenty of its cloud cover lingering at this time. Sharp clearing is \nnoted across northern IL which should set the stage for \nclearing skies this evening through the night. This loss of \ninsulation will sent temperatures into the low-to-mid 40s \ntonight. Despite the clear skies and recent rainfall, a \nnortheast wind near 5 knots may be just enough to prevent \nwidespread fog from developing. Yet patchy ground fog is \ncertainly plausible, as is common for October. \n\nHigh pressure shifts east through the weekend and a low races \nthrough interior Ontario as Sunday progresses. A trailing (and dry) \ncold front drops through Sunday afternoon, resulting in a late-day \ncooling trend into the 60s downwind of Lake Michigan. Any rain \nshowers appear confined to central Michigan and points north. In the \nwake of this front, a reinforcing dose of cold, Canadian high \npressure descends over the Midwest. Centered overhead Tuesday \nmorning, morning lows are on track to be in the mid-to-upper 30s. \n\nThis episode of cold air is shortlived as upper-level flow remains \nprogressive and ridging resumes late-week pushing afternoon highs \ninto the low-70s.",
